<h1 style="marign-top:10px;color:gray;font-size:24px;">Service Bulletin Rules</h1>

	<p>In case where, after the delivery of products, a failure or defect is detected, or an important 
	communication about the product has to be released, the manufacturing company concerned must then issue 
	a bulletin, and must absolutely contact directly the sales entities concerned in order to inform them and 
	plan the necessary retrofit operations.</p>
	
	<p>TLD official and exclusive communication media and implementation tracking system for service and 
	information bulletins is the TLD Web site Service Bulletin Module.</p>
	
	<p>They are two categories of bulletins:</p>

	<ol>
	
		<li><p><strong>SERVICE BULLETINS</strong>, with two levels :</p> 
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>COMPULSORY : A failure or defect has been detected, with a <span style="text-decoration:underline;">
					possible impact on safety</span>, or capacity of the equipment to perform its duty. 
					In this case, a compulsory service bulletin must be issued by the factory concerned, 
					the retrofit works must be carried out immediately by the sales entity, and all the 
					costs associated with such works (including labour) will be borne by the manufacturing 
					company.</p>
			  
			  		<p>During the warranty period, the remediation works must be carried out directly by the 
			  		sales entities (or if applicable by their subcontractors, under supervision of the sales 
			  		entities). After the warranty period, it is strongly advised that the remediation works are 
			  		carried out directly by the sales entities (or if applicable by their subcontractors, under 
			  		supervision of the sales entities). In case of difficulty to do so, it is accepted that the 
			  		implementation is made, by the sales entities, by way of sending the Service Bulletin and 
			  		relevant Parts to the final customer, but compulsorily with formal proof of acknowledgement 
			  		by the final customer.</p>
			
					<p>It has to be noted that all compulsory SB’s are transparently viewable by the customer 
					through the Extranet tools for a specific unit concerned by this SB.</p>
		 		</li>
				<li>
					<p>RECOMMENDED : A failure or defect has been detected, with <span style="text-decoration:underline;">
					no impact on safety</span> or capacity of the equipment to perform its duty, but with a possible impact on 
					reliability, performance, comfort or corporate image, that should be corrected. 
					In this case, a recommended service bulletin must be issued by the manufacturing 
					company concerned. the costs associated with such works are shared as follows :</p>
					
					<ul>
						<li>
							<p>Manufacturing companies shall pay for parts, and all related expenses (freight, duties...)</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>Sales entities or <span style="text-decoration:underline;">final customer</span>, 
							if this is the sales entity decision, shall bear the cost of labor, travel and 
							accommodation expenses of employees.</p>
						</li>
					</ul>  
		
					<p>The decision to carry out remediation works (to be done "TBD"), partially or on the entire fleet 
					of equipment concerned, is taken by each Sales and Service Organization Director, and needs 
					to be recorded in the service module. This decision has to be taken and recorded within one month 
					after the SB has been issued by the factory. Then, after the decision has been recorded, it is 
					becoming the responsibility of the Sales and Service entity to perform the remediation works or 
					information to the customer according to the original decision.</p>
			
					<p>All Recommended SB’s recorded as "to be done" by the SSO becomes immediately and transparently 
					accessible by the customer through the Extranet tools for a specific unit concerned by this SB.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	
		<li>
			<p><strong>INFORMATION BULLETINS</strong>, with 3 different possible categories</p>
			<ul>
				<li>OPERATION</li>
				<li>MAINTENANCE</li>
				<li>IMPROVEMENT</li>
			</ul>
	
			<p>For the IB, TLD does not have any obligation regarding their implementation in the fields vis-à-vis 
			its customers. Therefore, the TLD factory is not supposed to bear any cost regarding these IB’s. The 
			management of the IB’s and the accessibility of these IB’s on the extranet modules follows the same 
			rules than the recommended SB’s. Only the IB’s recorded as "TBD" will be accessible by the customer on 
			the extranet tools.</p>
			
			<p>It is important to note that after the SSO have been officially informed of a SB release, the SSO 
			<span style="text-decoration:underline;">has five working days</span> to start challenging the type of bulletin proposed by the factory 
			(SB or IB, Compulsory or Recommended) and/or the quality of the content of the SB.</p>
			
			<p>Although, after the bulletin release, the sales entities become responsible for its diligent 
			implementation in the field, in the case of a compulsory service bulletin involving safety exposure, each 
			manufacturing company has the responsibility to control and make sure that such bulletin has been 
			effectively and fully implemented in the fields by all the sales entities, before closing its corrective 
			action.</p>
		</li>
	</ol>

<h2>SB process and status:</h2>

	<p>The management of the SB is exclusively done through the TLD Intranet SB module.<br/>
	The SB process progresses through a series of phases, each represented by a specific status of the SB. 
	Each Status clearly defines which party is responsible (Factory or SSO)</p>

<h3>PENDING:</h3>

	<p>Prior to be issued, a Bulletin is prepared by the factory and shall have:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>Clear Title on which we will find equipment model and problem description</li>
		<li>Complete Documentation</li>
		<li>Comprehensive list of Parts required</li>
		<li>Full list of equipment affected</li>
	</ul>

	<p>Any Bulletin release by any factory has to generate an issuance warning information to the SSO Directors 
	concerned by this SB through a TLD workflow task by changing the Bulletin status from Pending to Approval.</p>

<h3>APPROVAL:</h3>

	<p>This is the phase during which the decision regarding whether a Bulletin is a SB or a IB, Compulsory 
	or Recommended and/or the quality of the content of the SB can be challenged by the SSO Directors.<br/>
	The SSO director has 5 working days to initiate the challenge with the factory. If the factory decision 
	has not been challenged within time, the factory is fully entitled to switch the SB status to the next one.</p> 

<h3>ER SELECTION:</h3><em>(for recommended SB and IB only, this status is automatically skipped for the Compulsory SB)</em>

	<p>This is the phase during which SSO Director shall make decision regarding the implementation of the 
	SB’s, particularly to record the "TBD" status for the recommended SB’s and IB’s.<br/>
	This action needs to be done within 30 days after the SB has been switched to Release status.</p>

<h3>IMPLEMENTATION:</h3>

	<p>This is the phase during which the implementation of the Bulletin is under the responsibility of 
	the SSO. All actions shall be done through the exclusive use of TLD website as per instructions 
	provided in "Service bulletin Management process" (see help page).</p>

<h3>CLOSED:</h3>

	<p>Once implementation plan is completed and recorded by SSO, factory shall change the SB status to 
	CLOSED. It is important to note that if switching the SB status to CLOSED is the responsibility of 
	the factory, the factory won’t be able to do it before all implementation by the SSO has been achieved 
	and recorded in the SB module.</p>

<p><em>Notes:</em><br/>The factory is not supposed to chase the SSO to insure the implementation of the SB, except the case of 
compulsory service bulletin involving safety exposure. Do remind that SB information would eventually appear on customer TLD extranet</p>


